      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      How do 3.2 billion base pairs of human DNA translate into phenotypic diversity, disease predisposition, and complex human traits? <strong>Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS)</strong> provide the foundational statistical and algorithmic bridge connecting population-scale genotype matrices to quantitative traits, cellular phenotypes, and clinical disease risk.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#ch1">1. Biological Variation & Paradigm Shift</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ch2">2. Microarrays & Statistical Imputation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ch3">3. Step-by-Step Quality Control (QC)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ch4">4. Mathematical Association Engine (OLS)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ch5">5. Confounding & Population Stratification</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ch6">6. Multiple Testing & Manhattan Plot</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ch7">7. Linkage Disequilibrium & Fine-Mapping</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ch8">8. Post-GWAS Functional Genomics</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ch9">9. Polygenic Risk Scores (PRS)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">10. Summary Cheat-Sheet & Visualizer</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Callout: Executive Summary -->
    <div class="deep-dive-callout">
      <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
        <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> Executive Summary & Core Mechanisms
      </div>
      <p>
        GWAS queries millions of genetic markers across hundreds of thousands of individuals using <strong>additive regression</strong>. To discover genuine biological signals, a GWAS must rigorously solve two major statistical challenges: <strong>population stratification confounding</strong> (via Ancestry PCA or Linear Mixed Models) and <strong>massive multiple testing</strong> (via the genome-wide Bonferroni threshold <Katex tex="p < 5 \times 10^{-8}" />).
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="ch1">
      <h2>1. Biological Variation & The Paradigm Shift to Association</h2>
      <p>
        The human diploid genome contains approximately <Katex tex="3.2 \times 10^9" /> base pairs. While any two unrelated humans share over 99.9% of their DNA sequence, the remaining 0.1% consists of millions of genetic variants that drive phenotypic diversity and disease susceptibility.
      </p>

      <h3>The Spectrum of Genetic Variation</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Single Nucleotide Polymorphisms (SNPs):</strong> Single base substitutions. Transitions (purine-to-purine <Katex tex="A \leftrightarrow G" /> or pyrimidine-to-pyrimidine <Katex tex="C \leftrightarrow T" />) occur roughly twice as frequently as transversions (<Katex tex="A/G \leftrightarrow C/T" />) due to spontaneous deamination of methylated cytosines.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Minor Allele Frequency (MAF):</strong> The frequency of the less common allele in a population. Variants are categorized as <strong>common</strong> (<Katex tex="\text{MAF} \ge 5\%" />), <strong>low-frequency</strong> (<Katex tex="1\% \le \text{MAF} < 5\%" />), or <strong>rare</strong> (<Katex tex="\text{MAF} < 1\%" />).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium (HWE):</strong> Under random mating and neutral drift, genotype frequencies at a biallelic locus with allele frequencies <Katex tex="p" /> (Ref) and <Katex tex="q = 1 - p" /> (Alt) follow the binomial expansion:
          <div style="margin: 0.5rem 0; text-align: center;">
            <Katex tex="p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1 \quad \implies \quad P(AA) = p^2, \; P(Aa) = 2pq, \; P(aa) = q^2" display={true} />
          </div>
          Significant deviations from HWE (<Katex tex="p_{\text{HWE}} < 10^{-6}" />) in control samples almost always indicate genotyping artifacts, batch hybridization failures, or severe non-random missingness.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The Historical Shift: Family Linkage vs. Population Association</h3>
      <p>
        During the 1980s and 1990s, human genetics was dominated by <strong>family-based linkage analysis</strong>. By tracking how chromosomal segments co-segregated through multi-generational pedigrees, researchers calculated Logarithm of Odds (LOD) scores to pinpoint single, highly penetrant mutations causing rare monogenic Mendelian disorders (e.g. <i>Huntington's disease</i>, <i>Cystic Fibrosis</i>, <i>Sickle Cell Anemia</i>).
      </p>
      <p>
        However, when linkage analysis was applied to common complex diseases (e.g. Type 2 Diabetes, Schizophrenia, Coronary Artery Disease, Crohn's disease), it failed almost completely. Complex diseases are not caused by single catastrophic coding mutations; rather, they follow the <strong>Common Disease–Common Variant (CDCV) hypothesis</strong>, where hundreds or thousands of common variants with subtle individual effect sizes (<Katex tex="\text{Odds Ratio} \approx 1.05 - 1.30" />) combine additively to shape disease liability. Uncovering this omnigenic architecture required shifting from small family pedigrees to <strong>unbiased population-scale association studies</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Dedicated Deep Dive: Biological Variation & The CDCV Paradigm</h3>
        <p>
          Explore the biophysics of mutation (transitions vs. transversions), Hardy-Weinberg derivation, LOD score mathematics, and the Pritchard Omnigenic Model in full detail.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/gwas-biological-variation-cdcv/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Read Full Deep Dive on Biological Variation</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="ch2">
      <h2>2. Genotyping Microarrays & Statistical Genotype Imputation</h2>
      <p>
        While whole-genome sequencing (WGS) of large cohorts was historically cost-prohibitive, <strong>SNP microarrays</strong> (such as Illumina Infinium and Affymetrix Axiom arrays) revolutionized human genetics by assaying 500,000 to 2,000,000 carefully chosen "tag SNPs" for under $50 per sample.
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Statistical Genotype Imputation via Hidden Markov Models (HMM)</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="P(\mathbf{G}_{\text{target}} \mid \mathbf{G}_{\text{chip}}, \mathbf{H}_{\text{ref}}) = \sum_{\text{paths}} P(\text{Transitions}) \cdot P(\text{Emissions})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          Tools like <strong>Beagle</strong>, <strong>Minimac4</strong>, and <strong>IMPUTE5</strong> treat target chip genotypes as noisy mosaic observations generated by meiotic copying from dense reference panels (such as the <strong>1000 Genomes Project</strong>, <strong>Haplotype Reference Consortium</strong>, and <strong>TOPMed</strong> with >100,000 sequenced genomes), boosting marker density from ~1 million to over <strong>15–30 million SNPs</strong>!
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Imputation yields a continuous dosage value <Katex tex="G_{ij} \in [0.0, 2.0]" /> representing the expected count of the minor allele:
        <Katex tex="G_{ij} = 0 \cdot P(G=0) + 1 \cdot P(G=1) + 2 \cdot P(G=2)" display={true} />
        Only well-imputed variants meeting the quality threshold (e.g. <Katex tex="R^2_{\text{info}} \ge 0.8" />) are retained for downstream association testing.
      </p>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Dedicated Deep Dive: SNP Microarrays & Statistical Imputation</h3>
        <p>
          Master the Li & Stephens genealogical copying model, HMM transition and emission formulations, PBWT scaling, and imputation quality metrics (<Katex tex="R^2_{\text{info}}" />).
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/gwas-genotyping-imputation/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Read Full Deep Dive on Genotype Imputation</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="ch3">
      <h2>3. Rigorous Step-by-Step Quality Control (QC) Protocols</h2>
      <p>
        A GWAS is only as good as its quality control. Without rigorous sample- and variant-level filtering, false-positive associations driven by technical batch artifacts will dominate the results.
      </p>

      <div class="qc-pipeline-grid">
        <div class="qc-card">
          <div class="qc-card-header">
            <Icon name="user-check" size={16} /> 1. Sample-Level Quality Control
          </div>
          <ul class="qc-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Sample Call Rate:</strong> Exclude any individual with genotype missingness <Katex tex="> 2\%" /> (call rate <Katex tex="< 98\%" />), which flags degraded DNA or poor hybridization.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Sex Discrepancy Check:</strong> Compare reported clinical sex against X-chromosome heterozygosity <Katex tex="F_{\text{inbreeding}}" /> (<Katex tex="F \approx 0" /> in females; <Katex tex="F \approx 1" /> in males) to detect sample swaps or Klinefelter/Turner syndromes.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Heterozygosity Outliers:</strong> Remove individuals with <Katex tex="|\text{Fhet}| > 3\text{ SD}" /> from the cohort mean (excess heterozygosity flags sample cross-contamination; deficit flags inbreeding).
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Cryptic Relatedness & Duplicates:</strong> Compute pairwise Identity-by-Descent (IBD). Remove one sample from every pair with kinship <Katex tex="\hat{\pi} = P(\text{IBD}=2) + 0.5 P(\text{IBD}=1) > 0.20" /> to maintain independent observations for OLS.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="qc-card">
          <div class="qc-card-header">
            <Icon name="sliders" size={16} /> 2. Variant-Level Quality Control
          </div>
          <ul class="qc-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Variant Missingness Rate:</strong> Exclude markers with missing call rates <Katex tex="> 2\%" /> across all retained individuals.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Minor Allele Frequency (MAF):</strong> Filter out rare variants (<Katex tex="\text{MAF} < 0.01" />) that lack statistical power and produce unstable standard errors in linear models.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium:</strong> Remove variants failing HWE goodness-of-fit (<Katex tex="p_{\text{HWE}} < 10^{-6}" /> in controls), which reliably flags probe binding flaws or copy number variation.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Differential Missingness:</strong> Remove variants showing significant call rate discrepancies between cases and controls (<Katex tex="p_{\text{diff}} < 10^{-5}" />).
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Dedicated Deep Dive: Rigorous Quality Control Protocols</h3>
        <p>
          Detailed mathematical explanations of sex chromosome inbreeding metrics (<Katex tex="F_{\text{inbreeding}}" />), autosomal heterozygosity outliers, pairwise IBD kinship estimation, and differential missingness tests.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/gwas-quality-control/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Read Full Deep Dive on Quality Control</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="ch4">
      <h2>4. The Mathematical Association Engine: OLS & Logistic Regression</h2>
      <p>
        Once the cleaned dosage matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{G} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times M}" /> is assembled, we evaluate the statistical association between each genetic variant <Katex tex="j" /> and the phenotype vector <Katex tex="\mathbf{y}" />.
      </p>

      <h3>Additive Genotype Dosage Encoding</h3>
      <p>
        The standard genetic formulation assumes an <strong>additive model</strong>, where each additional copy of the effect allele shifts the trait by <Katex tex="\beta_j" />:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Additive Genotype Dosage Encoding</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="G_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\} \quad \longrightarrow \quad \text{Count of Minor (Effect) Alleles}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="0 = \text{Ref/Ref (Homozygous reference)}" /> &emsp;|&emsp; 
          <Katex tex="1 = \text{Ref/Alt (Heterozygous)}" /> &emsp;|&emsp; 
          <Katex tex="2 = \text{Alt/Alt (Homozygous alternative)}" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Alternative non-additive models can also be evaluated:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Additive Model:</strong> <Katex tex="G_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\}" /> (each minor allele linearly shifts phenotype by <Katex tex="\beta_j" />).</li>
        <li><strong>Dominant Model:</strong> <Katex tex="G_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 1\}" /> (one copy is sufficient to confer full phenotypic effect).</li>
        <li><strong>Recessive Model:</strong> <Katex tex="G_{ij} \in \{0, 0, 1\}" /> (requires two copies of the minor allele).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Quantitative Traits: Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) Regression</h3>
      <p>
        For a continuous quantitative phenotype <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} \in \mathbb{R}^N" /> (e.g. standing height, LDL cholesterol, or fasting glucose), we formulate a single-variant Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression model:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Single-Variant Linear Regression Model</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="y_i = \alpha + \beta_j X_{ij} + \sum_{k=1}^K \gamma_k Z_{ik} + \epsilon_i, \quad \epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="X_{ij}" /> = Additive genotype dosage &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\beta_j" /> = Effect size per minor allele &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="Z_{ik}" /> = Covariates (Age, Sex, Ancestry PCs)
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Using the Frisch-Waugh-Lovell theorem to project out the covariate matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{Z}" />, the analytical Ordinary Least Squares estimate of the effect size <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_j" /> and its variance are given by:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">OLS Estimator, Standard Error & Statistical Power</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_j = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^N (X_{ij} - \bar{X}_j)(y_i - \bar{y})}{\sum_{i=1}^N (X_{ij} - \bar{X}_j)^2} = \frac{\text{Cov}(X_j, y)}{\text{Var}(X_j)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="\text{Var}(\hat{\beta}_j) = \frac{\sigma^2}{2 N p_j (1 - p_j)} \implies \text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_j) = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{2 N p_j (1 - p_j)}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="t_j = \frac{\hat{\beta}_j}{\text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_j)} \sim \text{Student's } t(df = N - K - 2) \quad \longrightarrow \quad p_j = 2 \cdot [1 - \Phi(|t_j|)]" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          Under Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium with minor allele frequency <Katex tex="p_j = \text{MAF}" />, the genotype variance is <Katex tex="\text{Var}(X_j) = 2p_j(1 - p_j)" />. Notice that statistical discovery power is directly proportional to <Katex tex="\sqrt{N \cdot p_j (1 - p_j) \cdot \beta_j^2}" />!
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Embedded Interactive Widget 1: Regression Sandbox -->
      <div class="embedded-widget">
        <div class="widget-header">
          <span>Interactive Sandbox: Single-Variant OLS Regression</span>
          <span style="font-size: var(--fs-meta); color: var(--color-muted);">Live Reactive Simulation</span>
        </div>
        <div class="widget-body">
          <div class="widget-viewport">
            <svg class="gwas-regr-svg" data-dd-regr-svg aria-label="Interactive Regression Plot"></svg>
          </div>
          <div class="widget-controls">
            <div class="widget-slider-group">
              <label for="dd-beta">Effect Size (&beta;):</label>
              <input id="dd-beta" type="range" min="-0.6" max="0.6" step="0.05" value="0.35" data-dd-beta-slider />
              <span class="widget-readout" data-dd-beta-val>+0.35</span>
            </div>
            <div class="widget-slider-group">
              <label for="dd-maf">Minor Allele Freq (MAF):</label>
              <input id="dd-maf" type="range" min="0.05" max="0.5" step="0.05" value="0.25" data-dd-maf-slider />
              <span class="widget-readout" data-dd-maf-val>25%</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem; text-align: center; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--color-ink);" data-dd-regr-stats>
            Slope β̂ = 0.350 | SE = 0.042 | t = 8.33 | p = 1.20e-13
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Case-Control Binary Traits: Logistic Regression & Odds Ratios</h3>
      <p>
        For binary clinical disease status <Katex tex="y_i \in \{0, 1\}" /> (e.g. Type 2 Diabetes case vs. control), we fit a Logistic Regression model via Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE):
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Logistic Regression & Odds Ratio (OR)</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\ln\left(\frac{P(y_i = 1)}{1 - P(y_i = 1)}\right) = \alpha + \beta_j X_{ij} + \sum_{k=1}^K \gamma_k Z_{ik}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="\text{Odds Ratio (OR)} = e^{\beta_j}, \quad 95\% \text{ CI} = \left[ e^{\hat{\beta}_j - 1.96 \cdot \text{SE}}, \; e^{\hat{\beta}_j + 1.96 \cdot \text{SE}} \right]" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Dedicated Deep Dive: The Mathematical Association Engine</h3>
        <p>
          Derive the complete Frisch-Waugh-Lovell projection, analytical standard error equations, IRLS Newton-Raphson logistic regression, and non-centrality power formulas.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/gwas-association-statistics/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Read Full Deep Dive on Association Statistics</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="ch5">
      <h2>5. Population Stratification & Confounding Conquered</h2>
      <p>
        The most notorious pitfall in statistical genetics is <strong>population stratification</strong>. In 1994, Eric Lander and Nicholas Schork described a classic thought experiment: imagine running a GWAS for <i>chopstick dexterity</i> in a mixed cohort of European and East Asian individuals living in San Francisco.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> The Chopsticks Problem & Spurious Association
        </div>
        <p>
          Chopstick usage is a cultural trait strongly correlated with East Asian ancestry. Simultaneously, thousands of completely unrelated alleles across the genome have divergent frequencies between Europe and East Asia due to historical genetic drift. A naive regression will find <strong>hundreds of thousands of false-positive "chopstick genes"</strong> across every chromosome!
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Mathematically, omitted ancestry <Katex tex="A" /> confounds the relationship because <Katex tex="\text{Cov}(X, A) \neq 0" /> and <Katex tex="\text{Cov}(y, A) \neq 0" />:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Mathematical Proof of Ancestry Confounding Bias</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\hat{\beta}_{\text{naive}}] = \beta_j + \frac{\text{Cov}(X_j, A)}{\text{Var}(X_j)} \cdot \gamma_{\text{Ancestry}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          When ancestry effect <Katex tex="\gamma_{\text{Ancestry}} \neq 0" />, the naive estimator is heavily biased away from 0 even if the true genetic effect <Katex tex="\beta_j = 0" />!
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Ancestry Principal Component Analysis (EIGENSTRAT)</h3>
      <p>
        To eliminate stratification, Price et al. (2006) introduced <strong>EIGENSTRAT</strong>. We construct the normalized genotype matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{G}^*" /> where each entry is standardized by its allele frequency <Katex tex="p_j" />:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Genotype Standardization & SVD Decomposition</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="G^*_{ij} = \frac{G_{ij} - 2p_j}{\sqrt{2p_j(1 - p_j)}} \quad \implies \quad \mathbf{G}^* = \mathbf{U} \mathbf{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^T" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{PC}_k = \mathbf{U}_k \cdot \sigma_k \quad (k = 1, \dots, 10)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          Top eigenvectors <Katex tex="\mathbf{PC}_1, \dots, \mathbf{PC}_{10}" /> capture continental and sub-continental ancestry axes. Including them as covariates in regression orthogonalizes the association test.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Linear Mixed Models (LMM: EMMAX / BOLT-LMM)</h3>
      <p>
        When cohorts contain cryptic relatedness, family structures, or complex population structure, <strong>Linear Mixed Models (LMM)</strong> replace fixed PC covariates with a random polygenic background effect:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Linear Mixed Model Formulation</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\beta + \mathbf{g}_j \gamma_j + \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{\epsilon}, \quad \mathbf{u} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_g^2 \mathbf{K}), \quad \mathbf{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_e^2 \mathbf{I})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{K} = \frac{1}{M} \mathbf{G}^* (\mathbf{G}^*)^T \quad \text{(Empirical Genetic Relationship Matrix)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\sigma_g^2" /> = Genetic variance component &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\sigma_e^2" /> = Environmental variance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="h^2_{\text{SNP}} = \frac{\sigma_g^2}{\sigma_g^2 + \sigma_e^2}" /> (Narrow-sense SNP Heritability)
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Genomic Inflation Factor (&lambda;_GC) & Q-Q Diagnostics</h3>
      <p>
        To audit whether a GWAS suffers from uncorrected confounding, we calculate the <strong>Genomic Inflation Factor (<Katex tex="\lambda_{GC}" />)</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Genomic Inflation Factor Formula</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\lambda_{GC} = \frac{\text{median}(\chi^2_{\text{obs}, 1}, \dots, \chi^2_{\text{obs}, M})}{0.454936}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="0.454936" /> is the theoretical median of a 1-degree-of-freedom Chi-Square distribution <Katex tex="\chi^2(df = 1)" /> under the null hypothesis. A pristine study yields <Katex tex="\lambda_{GC} \le 1.05" />.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Embedded Interactive Widget 2: PCA Confounding Simulator -->
      <div class="embedded-widget">
        <div class="widget-header">
